d is also floor((k+1)/2) but they gave a more detailed argument that the bound holds but may be less? Let's examine.\n\nFrom (2.4): number of admissible t ≤ floor((k-1)/|p+q|) + 1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). For odd k, k = 2m+1, so (k-1) = 2m, floor((2m)/|p+q|) + 1 ≤ floor((2m+2)/2) = m+1. So we need floor(2m/|p+q|) + 1 = m+1. This implies floor(2m/|p+q|) = m. So we need 2m/|p+q| ≥ m and m+1. So |p+q| ≤ 2 and > 2m/(m+1). For m≥1, 2m/(m+1) = 2 - 2/(m+1). So |p+q| must be > 2 - 2/(m+1). So for m≥1, this is > 1. So |p+q| must be > 1 and ≤ 2, so |p+q| = 2. So indeed for odd k≥3, to achieve the bound we need |p+q| = 2. For k=1 (m=0), the bound is floor((1+1)/2) = 1. For k=1, any line can contain at most 1 point, so any line works. But for k≥3 odd, we need |p+q|=2.\n\nThus the only possibilities for p,q with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q, and |p+q|=2 are indeed limited. Let's enumerate possibilities:\n\nCase 1: p+q = 2. Since q>0, p = 2 - q. Since gcd(p,q)=1, we need gcd(2-q,q)=1. Also p≠0 => q≠2. p≠ -q => 2 - q ≠ -q => 2 ≠ 0 => always true. So q can be 1 => p=1 => (p,q) = (1,1) => slope = p/q = 1. q=2 => p=0 => p=0 not allowed (horizontal line). q=3 => p=-1 => (p,q)=(-1,3) => gcd(1,3)=1, p≠0, p≠ -q => -1 ≠ -3 => ok. But p+q = -1+3 = 2, ok. So (p,q)=(-1,3) yields slope = -1/3. But is that allowed? Let's check p+q = 2 => p = 2 - q. For q=3, p = -1. So slope = p/q = -1/3. This is not parallel to x-axis (p≠0), not parallel to y-axis (q≠0), not parallel to x+y=0 (p≠ -q). So it's a sunny line. Does it have the property that it can contain (k+1)/2 points in T_k? Let's test for k=3 (m=1). For k=3, T_3 has points with x+y ≤ 4. The line with slope -1/3: equation p x - q y = c => -1 * x - 3 y = c? Actually p x - q y = c => p x - q y = c => -1*x - 3*y = c => -x - 3y = c => x + 3y = -c. Let's find integer points in T_3 that lie on this line. For small k, maybe it can contain at most floor((k+1)/2) = 2 points. Let's test if it can contain 2 points. For k=3, we need each line to contain exactly 2 points. Let's see if line x+3y = constant can have two points in T_3. For T_3, possible points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Compute x+3y for each: (1,1) => 4; (1,2) => 7; (1,3) => 10; (2,1) => 5; (2,2) => 8; (3,1) => 6. So possible values are 4,5,6,7,8,10. No two points share same x+3y value, so line x+3y = constant can contain at most 1 point. So slope -1/3 cannot achieve bound for k=3. For larger k, maybe it can contain more points? Let's test for k=5 (m=2). T_5 points have x+y ≤6. Compute x+3y values: many points. Could there be two points with same x+3y? Possibly. Let's see: (1,1) => 4; (2,1) => 5; (3,1) => 6; (4,1) => 7; (5,1) => 8; (1,2) => 7; (2,2) => 8; (3,2) => 9; (4,2) => 10; (1,3) => 10; (2,3) => 11; (3,3) => 12; (1,4) => 13; (2,4) => 14; (1,5) => 16. So we see duplicates: 7 appears for (4,1) and (1,2); 8 appears for (5,1) and (2,2); 10 appears for (4,2) and (1,3). So line x+3y = 7 contains (4,1) and (1,2). That's two points. So slope -1/3 line can contain at most 2 points for k=5. But bound for k=5 is floor((5+1)/2) = 3. So slope -1/3 cannot achieve bound for k=5. So indeed only slopes with |p+q|=2 and p,q small enough to achieve maximum points are those three: (p,q) = (1,1) => slope 1; (p,q) = (-2,1) => slope -2; (p,q) = (-1,2) => slope -1/2. Are there other possibilities? Let's enumerate all integer solutions to |p+q|=2 with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q.\n\nCase p+q = 2: p = 2 - q. q>0. p≠0 => q≠2. p≠ -q => 2 - q ≠ -q => 2 ≠ 0 => always true. So q can be 1 => p=1 (slope 1). q=3 => p=-1 (slope -1/3). q=4 => p=-2 (slope -2/4 = -1/2). Wait p=-2, q=4 => gcd(-2,4)=2 not 1, so not allowed. q=5 => p=-3 => gcd(-3,5)=1, slope -3/5. So many possibilities. But we also need p+q = -2 case.\n\nCase p+q = -2: p = -2 - q. q>0. p≠0 => -2 - q ≠ 0 => q ≠ -2 (always true). p≠ -q => -2 - q ≠ -q => -2 ≠ 0 => always true. So q can be 1 => p = -3 => slope -3. q=2 => p = -4 => slope -2. gcd(-4,2)=2 not allowed. q=3 => p = -5 => slope -5/3. So many possibilities.\n\nThus there are infinitely many (p,q) pairs with |p+q|=2 and gcd(p,q)=1. However, the solution claims only three possibilities: p=q=1 (slope 1), p=-2,q=1 (slope -2), p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2). But we just found other possibilities like (p,q)=(-1,3) (slope -1/3) and (p,q) = (1, -? Actually q>0, so (p,q) = (-3,1) slope -3, etc. So why are those not considered? Because they might not achieve the bound for all k? Let's examine.\n\nThe bound (2.4) is derived as floor((k-1)/|p+q|) + 1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). For |p+q|=2, the bound is floor((k-1)/2) + 1 = floor((k+1)/2). So any line with |p+q|=2 attains the bound? Not necessarily; the bound is an upper bound, but the actual number of points may be less due to other constraints (like the line may not intersect many points in the triangle). The solution claims that the bound is sharp (see §4) and that the only lines that achieve the bound are those three possibilities. But we need to verify if that's correct. For k=5, we saw that line with slope -1/3 can contain at most 2 points, not 3. So it does not achieve bound. So maybe only certain (p,q) pairs achieve the bound for all k. Let's examine the condition for a line to achieve the bound: we need the line to intersect the triangle T_k in exactly floor((k+1)/2) points. The line's integer points are given by (x0 + q t, y0 + p t). The sum x+y = (x0+y0) + (p+q) t. Since |p+q|=2, the sum changes by 2 each step. So the line will intersect the triangle at points where sum is within [2, k+1] (since x,y positive integers). So the number of integer t such that sum is in that interval is floor((k+1 - (x0+y0))/2) - ceil((2 - (x0+y0))/2) + 1? Something like that. The maximum number of points is achieved when the line's sum values align with the interval boundaries as tightly as possible. For a given line, the sum values are arithmetic progression with difference 2. So the maximum number of points is floor((k+1 - s_min)/2) + 1, where s_min is the smallest sum in the progression that is ≥2. To achieve floor((k+1)/2) points, we need s_min = 2 (or maybe 3?). Let's examine.\n\nIf the line's sum values start at 2, then the sums are 2,4,6,... up to ≤ k+1. For odd k, k+1 is even, so the last sum is k+1 (even). So the number of terms is (k+1 - 2)/2 + 1 = (k-1)/2 + 1 = (k+1)/2. So indeed if the line's sum values are exactly the even numbers from 2 to k+1, we get the maximum number of points. If the line's sum values are odd numbers (starting at 3), then the sums are 3,5,7,... up to ≤ k+1. For odd k, k+1 is even, so the last odd sum is k (since k is odd). Then number of terms = (k - 3)/2 + 1 = (k-1)/2 = floor((k+1)/2) - 1. So we lose one point. So to achieve the bound, the line's sum values must be even numbers. That means (x0+y0) must be even (since p+q = ±2). So we need the line to intersect the triangle at points where sum is even. So we need a line such that the sum parity matches. For p+q = 2, the sum increments by 2, so parity stays same. So if we start with an even sum, we get all even sums. If we start with odd sum, we get odd sums. So to achieve the bound, we need to start with sum = 2 (the smallest possible sum). That means there must be a point on the line with sum = 2, i.e., (1,1). So the line must pass through (1,1). Similarly, for p+q = -2, the sum decreases by 2 each step, but we can consider the same.\n\nThus the line must contain (1,1). Let's check the three lines given: y=x passes through (1,1). y=-2x+5 passes through (1,3)? Actually (1,3) sum=4, not (1,1). Wait line y=-2x+5: at x=1, y=3, sum=4. So not (1,1). But maybe the line passes through (2,1)? Actually (2,1) sum=3. Not (1,1). Let's check if line y=-2x+5 contains (3,1) sum=4. So sum is even. So the line's sum values are even numbers: (1,3) sum=4, (2,1) sum=3? Wait (2,1) is not on line: y = -2*2+5 = 1, yes (2,1) is on line, sum=3 (odd). So line includes both even and odd sums? Let's compute points on line y=-2x+5: integer points with x integer, y integer. For x=1 => y=3 sum=4 (even). x=2 => y=1 sum=3 (odd). x=3 => y=-1 sum=2 (but y negative, not in T_3). So within T_3, we have (1,3) sum=4, (2,1) sum=3. So the line includes both even and odd sums. But the bound for k=3 is floor((3+1)/2) = 2. So line can have at most 2 points, which it does. So it's okay.\n\nBut for larger k, we need each line to have exactly (k+1)/2 points. For k=5, we need each line to have 3 points. Let's see if line y=-2x+7 (the one for k=5) can have 3 points: points (1,5) sum=6, (2,3) sum=5, (3,1) sum=4. So sums are 6,5,4. That's three points. So line y=-2x+7 works for k=5. Indeed, for k=5, the line y=-2x+7 contains points (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). That's 3 points. So slope -2 line can achieve bound for any odd k? Let's test for k=7: line y=-2x+9 would have points (1,7),(2,5),(3,3),(4,1). That's 4 points, which is (7+1)/2 = 4. So slope -2 line works for any odd k: line y = -2x + (k+3)? Let's derive: For k odd, k = 2m+1, we need line with points (1, k+? Actually we need points (1, k?), (2, k-2?), (3, k-4?), ... until sum = k+1? Let's see: For k=2m+1, we want points (x, y) such that x+y = k+1? Actually the line y = -2x + (k+3) yields points: x=1 => y = -2 + (k+3) = k+1, sum = 1 + (k+1) = k+2 > k+1? Wait sum = k+2 > k+1, not allowed. Let's compute correctly: For k=5, line y = -2x + 7 gave points (1,5) sum=6 = k+1, (2,3) sum=5 = k, (3,1) sum=4 = k-1. So the line includes points with sums decreasing by 2 each step, starting from k+1 down to maybe 2? For k=5, we have sums 6,5,4. Actually sum decreases by 1? Wait from (1,5) sum=6, (2,3) sum=5, (3,1) sum=4. So sum decreases by 1 each step? But p+q = -2? Let's compute p and q for line y = -2x + c. Write as 2x + y = c. So p=2, q=1? Actually equation p x - q y = c => 2x - 1*y = c => p=2, q=1, p+q = 3, not 2. Wait we need to express line in form p x - q y = c. For y = -2x + c, rearrange: 2x + y = c => p=2, q=-1? Actually we need p x - q y = c, with q>0. So we can write 2x + y = c => p=2, q=-1? But q must be positive. Let's rewrite as -2x - y = -c => p = -2, q = 1? Actually p x - q y = c => p = -2, q = 1, then -2x - y = c => y = -2x - c, not correct. Let's find correct representation: y = -2x + c => bring to left: 2x + y = c => p=2, q=-1? Because p x - q y = c => 2x - (-1) y = c => q = -1, but q must be positive. So we can multiply both sides by -1: -2x - y = -c => p = -2, q = 1, c' = -c. Then p+q = -2 + 1 = -1, absolute value 1. That seems contradictory. Let's check the representation used in the solution: They wrote p x - q y = c, with q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q. For line y = -2x + c, we can write p = -2, q = 1, then p x - q y = -2x - y = c' => y = -2x - c'. But we want y = -2x + c. So set c' = -c. Then p x - q y = -2x - y = -c => multiply both sides by -1: 2x + y = c. But then p = 2, q = -1, which violates q>0. So the representation is not unique; we can multiply both sides by -1 to get p = 2, q = -1, but q must be positive. So we need to choose representation with q>0. So we can set p = -2, q = 1, c = -c (the constant). Then p+q = -2 + 1 = -1, absolute value 1. So for line y = -2x + c, p+q = -1, not 2. Wait that seems contradictory to earlier analysis that lines with slope -2 have |p+q|=2. Let's re-evaluate.\n\nGeneral line: slope m = p/q (since equation p x - q y = c => y = (p/q) x - c/q). So slope = p/q. For slope -2, we need p/q = -2 => p = -2q. Since gcd(p,q)=1, q must be 1, p = -2. So p = -2, q = 1. Then p+q = -2 + 1 = -1, absolute value 1. So indeed for slope -2, |p+q| = 1, not 2. Wait earlier we derived that lines achieving bound must have |p+q| = 2. But slope -2 lines have |p+q| = 1. So how can they achieve bound? Let's check the bound derivation: They derived bound (2.4) as floor((k-1)/|p+q|) + 1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). For |p+q| = 1, the bound becomes floor((k-1)/1) + 1 = (k-1) + 1 = k. So the bound is k, which is larger than floor((k+1)/2). But they also gave a more refined bound for |p+q| = 1: they argued that if |p+q| = 1 then p+q = ±1, and then they argued that q≥2 (otherwise p=0 which would give a horizontal line). Wait that argument seems to assume p+q = 1, then q≥2 because p = 1 - q, if q=1 then p=0, which is horizontal line (non-sunny). So for sunny lines with p+q = 1, we must have q≥2. So p = 1 - q, q≥2 => p negative. For p+q = -1, we have p = -1 - q, q>0 => p negative, q≥1. So p+q = -1 also possible. In those cases, |p+q| = 1, but they claim the bound is still floor((k+1)/2). Let's examine.\n\nIf p+q = 1, then p = 1 - q. Since q≥2, p ≤ -1. So slope = p/q = (1 - q)/q = 1/q - 1 = -(q-1)/q. So slope is negative, between -1 and 0 (since q≥2). For example, q=2 => p = -1 => slope = -1/2. That's one of the three lines. For q=3 => p = -2 => slope = -2/3. That's not one of the three lines. Does line with slope -2/3 achieve bound? Let's test for k=5. The line with slope -2/3: equation p x - q y = c => -2 x - 3 y = c? Actually p = -2, q = 3 => p+q = 1. So line is -2x - 3y = c => 2x + 3y = -c. Let's find integer points in T_5. For a line to have many points, we need to align with sum increments of 1? Actually p+q = 1, so sum changes by 1 each step. So the line can potentially intersect many points. But the bound they derived for |p+q| = 1 gave a refined bound floor((k+1)/2). Let's verify for slope -2/3.\n\nTake line 2x + 3y = C. For integer points, we need C ≡ 2x (mod 3) or something. Let's try to find a line that passes through many points in T_5. For k=5, T_5 includes points with x+y ≤6. Let's try to find line 2x + 3y = 8. Points: (1,2) => 2+6=8, sum=3; (2,2) => 4+6=10, not 8; (2,1) => 4+3=7; (3,1) => 6+3=9; (1,1) => 2+3=5; (2,3) => 4+9=13; (3,2) => 6+6=12; (4,1) => 8+3=11; (1,3) => 2+9=11; (2,4) => 4+12=16; (3,3) => 6+9=15; (4,2) => 8+6=14; (5,1) => 10+3=13. So not many points.\n\nLet's try line 2x + 3y = 7: (1,1) => 5; (1,2) => 8; (2,1) => 7 => (2,1) is a point. (3,1) => 9; (1,3) => 11; (2,2) => 10; (3,0) => 6 (y=0 not allowed). So only (2,1). So not many.\n\nThus slope -2/3 lines may not achieve bound.\n\nThus the solution's claim that only three lines achieve bound may be correct. But we need to verify if there are any other lines that can achieve bound for odd k. Let's examine systematically.\n\nWe need a sunny line that contains exactly floor((k+1)/2) points in T_k. For odd k, that's (k+1)/2 points. The line's integer points are given by (x0 + q t, y0 + p t). The sum x+y = (x0+y0) + (p+q) t. Since p+q = ±2 (to achieve bound), the sum changes by 2 each step. So the line's points in T_k will have sums forming an arithmetic progression with difference 2. To have (k+1)/2 points, we need the progression to include all sums from 2 to k+1 (inclusive) that have the same parity (either all even or all odd). Since there are (k+1)/2 numbers of each parity in that range (for odd k, there are (k+1)/2 even numbers and (k+1)/2 odd numbers). So the line must contain either all even sums or all odd sums. So the line must have sum parity constant (which is true) and must start at the smallest sum of that parity (2 for even, 3 for odd). So the line must contain a point with sum = 2 (if even) or sum = 3 (if odd). But sum = 2 corresponds to point (1,1). Sum = 3 corresponds to points (1,2) or (2,1). So the line must pass through (1,1) or (1,2) or (2,1). Let's examine possibilities.\n\nCase 1: line passes through (1,1). Then sum = 2 (even). The line's sum progression will be even numbers: 2,4,6,... up to k+1 (which is even). So the line will contain exactly (k+1)/2 points if it stays within the triangle. So any line through (1,1) with |p+q|=2 will achieve bound. Let's find all such lines.\n\nWe need p+q = ±2, and line passes through (1,1). The line equation p x - q y = c. Plug (1,1): p - q = c. So c = p - q. So line is p x - q y = p - q => p (x-1) = q (y-1). So (x-1)/(y-1) = q/p. So slope = p/q = (x-1)/(y-1). Actually slope = p/q, but we can find p,q such that line passes through (1,1). The line through (1,1) with slope m = p/q is y - 1 = (p/q)(x - 1). So any rational slope works. But we need p+q = ±2. So we need integer p,q with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q, and p+